A||||5||Boodoggin||Shift Kit Questions||||1||
Z||000000||Boodoggin||06-04-2000||11:28 AM||||Hey Guys! Im just learning about cars and I was hoping one of you can explain shift kits to me.  How do they work?  Are they easy to install?  What exactly do they do?  Do any of you have them?  Do you like them?<P>Thanks Alot<BR>Mark B.||||reg||1
Z||000001||sdm1964||06-05-2000||08:23 AM||||Ok the basics, On a stock transmission the clutches slowly engage for smoothness. This makes for an easy shifting car but lets the cluthes slip during engagement. A shift kit makes the cluthes firmly engage, hence less wear on the clutches and bands. To install one you have to remove the trans pan and valve body. There are little check balls so be carefull. You either remove, replace or rearrange the balls. Sometimes have to drill another hole, Details will be included with the kit. It is fairly simple. I do not have one on my GT, I like it the way it is.<p>[This message has been edited by sdm1964 (edited 06-05-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000002||Boodoggin||06-05-2000||10:52 AM||||sdm, thanks that helped a lot!||||reg||14
Z||000003||Dan||06-05-2000||06:45 PM|||| If you have a v6 there is a throttle valve cable that is adjustable which hooks onto the throttle.After adjusting, my shifts are much stiffer and shifts and kicksdown at higher rpm.It adjusts the tranny shift valve pressure.Mucher morer fun to drive.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula||06-05-2000||08:49 PM||||Dan, can you explain this more for me because I think monkeyman wants to know more.<P>(or maybe I do)||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||06-05-2000||10:00 PM||||I'm no tranny expert, but owning a Thunderbird has taught me a lot about them.  The guys in the 89-up T-bird club used to swear by a company called Trans-Go, with Baumann Engineering being the close favorite for brands. Read the tech bbs at TCCOA.com if you want to see some real automatic tranny woes.  A friend of mine has a B&M kit with their Trickshift ATF in his 88 Formula 350, and he loves it.<P>A few guys in the club have used a plug-in module that raises the line pressure, which is controlled by the computer on newer trannies, causing firmer shifts.  Unfortunately, a few of them have had tranny problems soon after, so be careful about screwing with the line pressure in your tranny, since this can cause distortion and leaks in your valve body.  I would be careful about the throttle valve cable adjustment if it affects line pressure.<BR>  <BR>My understanding of most shift kits is that it changes the rate at which the accumulator pistons fill, by changing springs, check balls, drilling/enlarging passages, etc.  Since the accumulators control the shifting, this changes the shift characteristics.  It seems that most heavy duty usage vehicles can benefit from a firmer shift, due to less slippage (read: heat/wear), but there can be too much of a good thing, too, because of the added stress on other driveline components.  The factory thinks we all want our cars to shift like Buick Park Avenues, so that's what we're given to start with. <P>My $.02, and I'm thankful for my Getrag!  Wish I could put a T-45 in my 'bird.||||reg||1
